TY  - CHAP
AU  - Gobber, Giovanni
A1  - Bigi, S
TI  - The Functions of Communication in Medical Encounters
T2  - COMMUNICATING (WITH) CARE: A LINGUISTIC APPROACH TO THE STUDY OF DOCTOR-PATIENT INTERACTIONS
M3  - Article
M3  - Book Chapter
AB  - The models describing the doctor-patient relationship have mainly placed their attention on the roles of the participants and on the goals of the interaction. Communication is considered from the point of view of the aims it contributes to achieve and is generally described in terms of three main functions: rapport building; information exchange; and decision making. The treatment of these functions in the health communication literature is reviewed with the aim of highlighting issues that still need to be clarified in order to provide clear indications to clinicians and policy makers on how to improve doctor-patient interactions. One of the aspects that is more difficult to clarify is the interplay between communication and context, in particular the pathways that lead from communication functions to patient outcomes. As the final section explains, this connection can be clarified only by referring to a comprehensive model of communication.
