TY  - CHAP
AU  - Gobber, Giovanni
A1  - Bigi, S
TI  - The Conceptualization of Communication Underlying Different Paradigms of Care
T2  - COMMUNICATING (WITH) CARE: A LINGUISTIC APPROACH TO THE STUDY OF DOCTOR-PATIENT INTERACTIONS
M3  - Article
M3  - Book Chapter
AB  - The chapter reviews the main paradigms of healthcare described in the literature, highlighting how communication is understood within each framework. Whereas these paradigms focus on the roles of and relationship between doctors and patients, understanding of the communicative process remains unclear. A recurrent observation in studies on communication in healthcare is that the tools for assessing the quality of the interaction are too many, too diverse and often atheoretical, meaning that they are not clearly based in a theory of interaction. As the assumptions on which they are designed are not clear, there is often confusion regarding the variables they are trying to measure. The result is that a great quantity of empirical evidence is produced, which cannot be used as a reliable base to design interventions. The concluding section summarizes the main weaknesses of assessment tools determined by such undeterminacy regarding communication and points out the need for an interdisciplinary approach to the study of doctor-patient interactions.
