TI  - Humor During Clinical Practice: Analysis of Recorded Clinical Encounters
T2  - JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN BOARD OF FAMILY MEDICINE
M3  - Article
AB  - Objective: Little is known about humor's use in clinical encounters, despite its many potential benefits. We aimed to describe humor during clinical encounters.Design: We analyzed 112 recorded clinical encounters. Two reviewers working independently identified instances of humor, as well as information surrounding the logistics of its use.Results: Of the 112 encounters, 66 959%) contained 131 instances of humor. Humor was similarly frequent in primary care 936/61, 59%) and in specialty care 930/51, 59%), was more common in gender-concordant interactions 943/63, 68%), and was most common during counseling 981/112, 62%). Patients and clinicians introduced humor similarly 963 vs 66 instances). Typically, humor was about the patient's medical condition 940/131, 31%).Discussion and Conclusion: Humor is used commonly during counseling to discuss the patient's medical condition and to relate to general life events bringing warmth to the medical encounter. The timing and topic of humor and its use by all parties suggests humor plays a role in the social connection between patients and physicians and allows easier discussion of difficult topics. Further research is necessary to establish its impact on clinicians, patients, and outcomes.
