TY  - JOUR
AU  - PLANTINGA, LAURA
TI  - UTILITY OF A FUNCTIONING REPORT FOR LUPUS PATIENTS AND THEIR PROVIDERS
M3  - Awarded Grant
AB  - Background: Functional impairment is associated with older age, but even relatively young patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) have reported substantial physical and cognitive functional impairment. Tools to improve the recognition or facilitate the discussion of functioning, specifically in the SLE population, are lacking. To address this gap, we will leverage existing detailed physical and cognitive functioning data from a recently completed pilot study of SLE patients [Approaches to Positive, Patient-Centered Experiences of Aging in Lupus (APPEAL)], as well as an existing, app-based, patient-friendly physical functioning report that is being used in an ongoing study in another chronic disease population.Focus Areas: This biopsychosocial and epidemiological study contributes to the first FY17 LRP Focus Area (understanding of lupus disease heterogeneity) by providing a strategy/technology to subtype patients, based on level of functioning; the functioning report also provides a means to delivery of personalized medicine and insight into the variation in treatment and its effects on patient-centered outcomes in SLE.Objectives: Our objectives are to determine whether an existing physical functioning report has applicability in the SLE population and to determine patient and provider preferences for content and presentation of additional information on cognitive functioning in this population.Specific Aims: Our aims are: (1) to assess, in a pilot sample of SLE patients, the utility of our physical functioning report in patient-provider encounters, and (2) to determine whether and how cognitive assessment data should be included in a functioning report for SLE patients.Innovation: This proposal is innovative in its emphasis on patient-centered assessments, which include nondisease-specific outcomes, such as functioning, that are often prioritized by patients over traditional clinical outcomes. The proposed study is a first step in exploring the value of patient-centered assessments to improve risk stratification, develop individualized treatment plans, maximize treatment adherence, and assess the impact of disease-specific treatment on patient-reported outcomes, specifically in SLE. The proposed study will also use measures of functioning that are common in geriatric patients but novel among SLE patients. Additionally, a personalized, multi-domain report of a patient's functioning could prove invaluable in improving patient and provider recognition of issues and influencing patient-centered communication. Finally, data from this study could be used to design future studies that include real-time measurement and report generation; longitudinal assessment of functioning via the report; and examination of the effect of the report on patient-physician communication and shared decision-making, patient satisfaction with and trust of the provider, treatment adherence, and both clinical and patient-reported outcomes.Impact: This study will inform whether and how the delivery of information about functioning can be optimized for SLE patients and providers and also guide a future study assessing effectiveness and feasibility of the report in the clinical setting, potentially improving SLE patient-provider communication and treatment adherence, as well as delaying or preventing further declines in functioning among SLE patients.Research Strategy: To address Aim 1, we will make minor modifications to the report and the existing Android app underlying it, to ensure compatibility with existing APPEAL pilot data on multiple domains related to physical functioning. Then, using existing APPEAL study data and the modified app, we will generate and send individualized physical functioning reports to the 60 APPEAL participants. Finally, we will survey the patients regarding the utility of the report and incorporate this feedback into refinements of the app and report and in future study planning. To address Aim 2, we will solicit qualitative feedback on the potential content and visualization of a report using cognitive functioning data from patient and SLE provider focus groups, which will be incorporated into refinements of the app and report and in future study planning.
